// Package jsonv2 is the JSON layer of the module cdproto. It names the types
// and the funcs of version 2 of the JSON package that the generated code and
// the programs that use it need, with the same names as the standard library.
//
// The generated code reads and writes JSON with the packages encoding/json/v2
// and encoding/json/jsontext. Go 1.27 has both packages in the standard library.
// Go 1.25 and Go 1.26 have them only when the GOEXPERIMENT environment variable
// is set to jsonv2. A program that does not set it cannot import them. To
// support these versions of Go, this package is the only place that imports a
// JSON package. It imports no other package of the module, so every other
// package can import it.
//
// The files json_std.go and json_compat.go define the same names. The build
// chooses one of them:
//
//   - json_std.go builds with Go 1.27 or later, and with GOEXPERIMENT=jsonv2.
//     It uses the packages of the standard library. Its types are aliases of the
//     standard types, so a program on Go 1.27 can pass a [Value] to any
//     function that takes a jsontext.Value.
//   - json_compat.go builds with all other versions of Go. It uses the module
//     github.com/go-json-experiment/json, which is the source of the standard
//     packages. The module is pinned to a version that needs Go 1.25.
//
// The build tag cdproto_jsoncompat selects json_compat.go on every version of
// Go. Use it to test the second file with a newer toolchain. Go 1.27 turns the
// jsonv2 experiment on by default, and the pinned module does not build with
// the standard package of Go 1.27. On Go 1.27, set GOEXPERIMENT=nojsonv2 with
// the tag.
//
// The two files must behave the same. Both use the default options of version 2
// of the JSON package, and neither one changes them.
package jsonv2
